1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,890
You walk into a room...

2
00:00:02,890 --> 00:00:04,440
and forget why you came in.

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:05,980
That's not a bad memory.

4
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:08,540
Scientists say the doorway itself wiped

5
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,240
it.

6
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:11,550
Your brain is full of glitches like this.

7
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:13,930
Songs that won't leave, words you can't

8
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:16,300
say, a phone that buzzes when it didn't.

9
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:18,250
Every one of them has been tested in

10
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,430
a lab. And every glitch turns out to

11
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:22,750
be a shortcut, working a little too hard.

12
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:24,550
So let's start with that doorway.

13
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:28,580
In 2011, psychologist Gabriel Radvansky,

14
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:30,700
at the University of Notre Dame, built a

15
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:32,840
set of virtual rooms, like a simple video

16
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:33,540
game.

17
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,800
Volunteers picked up objects, carried them

18
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,060
from table to table, and were quizzed on

19
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:39,480
what they had just been holding.

20
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:41,550
People who walked through a doorway forgot

21
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,410
more than people who walked the same

22
00:00:43,410 --> 00:00:45,190
distance inside one room.

23
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:47,360
Then his team tried it in real rooms,

24
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:48,200
with real boxes.

25
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:49,590
Same result.

26
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:52,550
The idea: your brain cuts life into

27
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,390
scenes, like chapters in a book.

28
00:00:54,790 --> 00:00:56,770
A doorway tells it, new chapter.

29
00:00:57,130 --> 00:00:58,650
And the plan from the last chapter gets

30
00:00:58,650 --> 00:00:59,610
filed away.

31
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:03,130
But in 2021, a team in Australia couldn't

32
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:04,150
always repeat it.

33
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:06,390
The doorway only tripped people up when

34
00:01:06,390 --> 00:01:07,800
their minds were already busy.

35
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:09,770
Which is exactly how you walk into the

36
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:11,710
kitchen... thinking about ten other

37
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:12,190
things.

38
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:16,480
Next glitch. A song gets stuck in your

39
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,520
head, and it plays on repeat for hours.

40
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:19,980
Why?

41
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:22,100
Scientists call it an earworm.

42
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,160
In a survey of almost twelve thousand

43
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:26,040
people in Finland, more than ninety

44
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,950
percent said they get one every week.

45
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:30,830
In 2016, researchers in London asked three

46
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:32,850
thousand people which songs got stuck the

47
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:33,550
most.

48
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:35,480
The winners had a lot in common: a

49
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,460
fast tempo, and a simple tune that climbs

50
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,480
up and comes back down, like a nursery

51
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,180
rhyme.

52
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,480
Plus a few unusual jumps in the melody

53
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:44,040
that your brain didn't see coming.

54
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:45,710
And here's a strange fix.

55
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:48,270
At the University of Reading, chewing gum

56
00:01:48,270 --> 00:01:50,130
cut how much people heard the song in

57
00:01:50,130 --> 00:01:51,750
their head by about a third.

58
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:53,840
Chewing keeps busy the same inner voice

59
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:55,750
your brain uses to sing to itself.

60
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:58,050
So the next time a song won't leave...

61
00:01:58,050 --> 00:01:58,450
try gum.

62
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:01,220
You know the word.

63
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:02,680
You can feel it.

64
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,460
It starts with...

65
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:06,150
something. But it just won't come out.

66
00:02:06,490 --> 00:02:07,910
That's the tip-of-the-tongue feeling.

67
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:11,970
In 1966, two Harvard psychologists, Roger

68
00:02:11,970 --> 00:02:14,190
Brown and David McNeill, decided to cause

69
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:15,280
it on purpose.

70
00:02:15,610 --> 00:02:17,230
They read out the definitions of rare

71
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:19,190
words, and waited for students to get

72
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:19,890
stuck.

73
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:21,580
Many did, right on the edge of the

74
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:22,280
answer.

75
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:23,750
And while they were stuck, they could

76
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:26,010
often guess the first letter, and how many

77
00:02:26,010 --> 00:02:27,200
syllables the word had.

78
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:28,960
So the word isn't lost.

79
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,260
Your brain has found the meaning, but it

80
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:32,720
can't fetch the sound.

81
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:34,870
Like a library card that points to a

82
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:36,540
book... that's out on loan.

83
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:39,280
Try it: what's the little plastic tip on

84
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:40,500
the end of a shoelace called?

85
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:42,920
It's an aglet.

86
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:44,840
Was it on the tip of your tongue?

87
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:48,610
Now picture a loud party.

88
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:51,270
Music, chatter, clinking glasses.

89
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:53,320
You're deep in one conversation...

90
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:55,850
and then, from across the room, someone

91
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:56,630
says your name.

92
00:02:57,030 --> 00:02:58,130
And you hear it instantly.

93
00:02:58,830 --> 00:02:59,530
How?

94
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:02,250
In 1953, the British scientist Colin

95
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:04,390
Cherry played two different messages, one

96
00:03:04,390 --> 00:03:06,610
in each ear, and asked people to repeat

97
00:03:06,610 --> 00:03:07,440
only one.

98
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,700
Afterwards, they could barely say a thing

99
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:10,580
about the other ear.

100
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,260
They didn't even notice when it switched

101
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:13,180
to another language.

102
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:16,870
But in 1959, Neville Moray hid each

103
00:03:16,870 --> 00:03:19,210
listener's own name in the ignored ear.

104
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:20,960
About a third of them heard it.

105
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,340
When scientists repeated the test in 1995,

106
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:25,560
the answer was the same: about one in

107
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:26,260
three.

108
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:28,630
So your brain isn't ignoring the noise.

109
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:31,270
It's quietly listening to all of it, and

110
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:33,140
lets through what matters most to you.

111
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:35,350
And few words matter more to you than

112
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:36,110
your own name.

113
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,040
Look at this power socket.

114
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:40,870
Did you just see a face?

115
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:43,090
Seeing faces in things is called

116
00:03:43,090 --> 00:03:46,030
pareidolia: in clouds, in the front of a

117
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:48,010
car, in a slice of pizza.

118
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:50,910
In 2009, a team at Harvard Medical School

119
00:03:50,910 --> 00:03:52,930
recorded people's brains while they looked

120
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:54,340
at face-like objects.

121
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:56,780
The brain's face area fired in about a

122
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:58,980
sixth of a second, almost as fast as

123
00:03:58,980 --> 00:03:59,920
for a real face.

124
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:01,930
Plain objects didn't do it.

125
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:04,510
So you don't decide to see the face.

126
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:06,760
It's spotted before you even think.

127
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,640
In 2020, scientists at the US National

128
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,420
Institute of Mental Health watched it

129
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:12,730
happen in more detail.

130
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:15,300
For a split second, the brain handles the

131
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,660
fake face like a real one.

132
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,400
Then it quickly files it away as an

133
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:19,100
object.

134
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:21,830
One idea why: a face detector that's a

135
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:23,970
bit too eager is safer than one that

136
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:24,990
misses a real face.

137
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:28,120
Your phone buzzes in your pocket.

138
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:29,760
You check. Nothing.

139
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:31,770
No message, no call.

140
00:04:32,070 --> 00:04:33,590
If that's happened to you, you're in the

141
00:04:33,590 --> 00:04:34,290
majority.

142
00:04:34,510 --> 00:04:36,850
In 2010, a survey at a US hospital

143
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:38,750
found that sixty-eight percent of the

144
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:40,710
staff had felt phantom vibrations.

145
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:43,270
In 2012, a study of two hundred and

146
00:04:43,270 --> 00:04:45,090
ninety students put it at eighty-nine

147
00:04:45,090 --> 00:04:45,790
percent.

148
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:48,050
The likely reason: your brain is waiting

149
00:04:48,050 --> 00:04:48,790
for that buzz.

150
00:04:49,210 --> 00:04:51,110
So it's set to better safe than sorry.

151
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:54,080
A tiny muscle twitch, or clothes rubbing

152
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:56,260
your skin, and the brain shouts: phone!

153
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,420
In the hospital study, people who carried

154
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:00,360
their phone for longer, or used vibrate

155
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,650
mode more, felt more phantom buzzes.

156
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,420
And over ninety percent of the students

157
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:05,720
said it barely bothered them.

158
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:07,940
It's just a very eager brain.

159
00:05:09,510 --> 00:05:11,310
And the strangest glitch of all.

160
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:13,930
You walk into a place you've never been...

161
00:05:13,930 --> 00:05:16,010
and you're sure you've been here before.

162
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:18,370
That's déjà vu, and surveys suggest more

163
00:05:18,370 --> 00:05:20,240
than half of all people have felt it.

164
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:23,030
In 2012, psychologist Anne Cleary, at

165
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:25,250
Colorado State University, sent volunteers

166
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:27,130
into a virtual town built in a

167
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:28,530
life-simulation game.

168
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:30,710
Some new scenes had the same layout as

169
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:32,670
scenes they had seen before, but with

170
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:33,590
different things inside.

171
00:05:34,030 --> 00:05:36,260
A new room, in an old room's shape.

172
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:38,540
Those were the scenes that set off déjà

173
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:40,560
vu, even when people couldn't remember the

174
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:41,500
first room at all.

175
00:05:41,870 --> 00:05:44,030
Then in 2016, a team at the University

176
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:46,410
of St Andrews in Scotland caught déjà vu

177
00:05:46,410 --> 00:05:47,830
inside a brain scanner.

178
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:49,920
The busy areas weren't the memory stores.

179
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:51,480
They were at the front of the brain,

180
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,100
in the parts that check memories for

181
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:53,820
mistakes.

182
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:55,960
So déjà vu may be your brain catching

183
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:58,860
a false alarm: this feels familiar...

184
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:00,070
but wait, it isn't.

185
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:01,820
Some scientists think that's a good sign:

186
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,760
a memory that double-checks itself.

187
00:06:05,410 --> 00:06:07,670
So: the doorway, the stuck song, the

188
00:06:07,670 --> 00:06:10,010
missing word, your name in the noise, the

189
00:06:10,010 --> 00:06:12,410
face in the socket, the phantom buzz, and

190
00:06:12,410 --> 00:06:13,160
déjà vu.

191
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:14,760
They feel like your brain is broken.

192
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:17,360
But each one is a shortcut that usually

193
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,680
helps you, caught working a little too

194
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:20,380
hard.

195
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:22,110
Which glitch happens to you the most?

196
00:06:22,490 --> 00:06:23,420
Tell us in the comments.

197
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:25,910
Now... what did you come here for again?
